            <ArticleTitle>Patient outcomes after reoperation on the lumbar spine.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>706-11</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A consecutive series of thirty-nine patients who had had a reoperation of the lumbar spine was followed up for an average of forty-eight months (range, twenty-four to eighty-six months). The patients were evaluated with regard to pain, functional status, and work status. Twenty-eight patients (72 percent) had a successful outcome, as determined by their ability to return to work, their lack of a need for narcotic analgesics, and their satisfaction with the operative results. Factors that were significantly associated with a successful outcome included a younger age (p &lt; 0.02), working outside the home (p &lt; 0.05), an initial period of improvement after the previous (index) operation (p &lt; 0.01), fewer spinal levels operated on previously (p &lt; 0.05), and a revision procedure incorporating anterior interbody arthrodesis (p &lt; 0.02).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
